A||||7||Robert||Security battery......?||||5||
Z||000000||Robert||04-07-2000||10:35 AM||||Has anyone heard of or tried the DieHard Security battery?  I noticed it in a newspaper ad this week; it claims, “Anti-theft function immobilizes your vehicle with a click of the remote control”.  Cost is about double of the regular DieHard, $147.  Sounds interesting.||||reg||5
Z||000001||lennyb4||04-07-2000||11:17 AM||||Honestly they do work. I restored a 1970 MG for my wife and i just installed one in it about a month ago. The car is candy apple red w/metal flake and in showroom condition and i wanted to replace the alarm system (thing gets on my nerves). It comes with a remote control. What it does is it stops the flow of electricity. Kinda works like a ignition kill switch.Im planning on getting another one for my Fiero as soon as im done with the resto.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-07-2000||12:23 PM||||These are built by Johnson Controls for Sears. (I believe Johnson is currently contracted for all Diehard applications) Other JCI customers will likely have a version of it soon. You can see who many of JCI's customers are at <A HREF="http://www.jci.com" TARGET=_blank>www.jci.com</A> <P>It does a couple things.<P>It monitors the charging system. When the car is shut off, and you do something dumbass like leave the lights on the battery brain reduces current flow after a few minutes to prevent killing the battery.<P>It also has the remote kill control that puts the battery in low current mode to prevent engine starting. It won't even allow jumping in this mode acording to Sears.<P>It does all this and still flows enough current to preserve the ECM and radio clock.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BOILERMAKER||04-07-2000||03:20 PM||||I just quit JCI.  I knew a little about these batteries and that we made them, but I was in a different branch of JCI.  I had to go all the way up to WI to get one at less cost, so I didn't even see any benefit from this product.<P>I've heard that they work really well though.<P>Go figure!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||WGraffiKs||04-07-2000||03:53 PM||||I saw an ad for these batteries, and at first glance, it seems like a good idea.  The battery is considerably more costly than a regular bat, tho.  So here's the question... What happens in 72 months when the battery portion goes bad?  Another 1.5 hundred dollars to replace, or can the 'brains' part be retained?  Anyone know?<BR>After MY battery goes bad, my alarm is reusable. (LOL!)<p>[This message has been edited by WGraffiKs (edited 04-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||04-07-2000||05:55 PM||||The battery may stop a car thief from starting the car but what about just dragging it up a flatbed and driving away? I prefer regular car alarms. Based on statistics if a pro-thief wants your car it's gone. No matter what kind of alarm/security devices you have. If you could bolt your car to the street they would take the street! The regular car alarms keeps out the riff raff that just wants to steal whats inside or the novice car thief who wants to joyride your car into a ditch. Most alarm systems now incorporate an anti-carjack mode too. With a regular security system if the car is stolen, chances are you won't see it because by the time the police start to track it it's in a million pieces being sold as used parts. If you really want an ignition kill for the vehicle Radio Shack sells a switch for $.69. The best way to install an alarm system is to do it yourself and make access to the "brain box" as difficult as possible. Fact is all you have to do is delay the thief for 60 seconds. If he can't get it going by then, the risk of him getting caught goes up drastically. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||04-07-2000||08:54 PM||||Orief,<P>Depends on where you are. It's not at all uncomon to see wreckers in NYC with cars on the hook and alarms squalling. (Mostly they are NYC police tow wreckers But who really pays attention....... Most people just want to not be bothered by the constant false alarms that most times they are.)<P>generally I agree tho. I've though of an ignition killer in mine. Just an issue of figuring out the best place to kill DIS without frying the 100+$us PIM.<P>WG, <P>after the 3 year full replacement the warranty reverts to the normal prorated every other battery has. No, you can't move the brain. it's totally built in.<P>In truth I've never had a battery last 3 years. Something always kills it by then. It will be interesting to see what number of batteries JCI and Sears eat on this one.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||04-07-2000||10:07 PM||||The best spot for the ignition kill is the starter wire that goes from the ignition switch to the starter. Please note that a manual car can be push started. The other way is the power wire to the ignition switch. This way you wont mess up DIS or ECM. If you really want to do a quick way, Just add a piece of tape to the fuel pump fuse, When you park in an unfamilar area just pull the fuse! Most thieves won't bother looking for a missing fuse. ||||reg||1
